  "slug": "dismutase-history-horizon-d1a8",
  "summary": "New Arabidopsis, apple, pear and soybean studies map redox signals onto specific protein modules controlling stomata, germination, photoprotection and fruit traits.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is redox chemistry acting through defined protein modules rather than as nonspecific damage. In Arabidopsis, peroxisomal H2O2 homeostasis under PEG-induced water stress rebalances ABA and GA metabolism, slowing seed germination. 2-Cys peroxiredoxins interact with FAD7/FAD8 to raise trienoic fatty acids in phosphatidylglycerol and aid PSII recovery from high light. H2S persulfidation and PRMT5-dependent LSm4 methylation connect H2S signaling to alternative splicing in stomatal closure. In apple, MxMPK4-1 phosphorylates MxME3-7 at S201, enhancing ROS signaling under iron deficiency. In pear, PUB8 targets ZAT11/12 for degradation, while ZAT11/12 activate LAC1 to drive ROS-linked lignin polymerization in stone cells. Ascorbate regulation by MdMYB70/MdbHLH62 in fresh-cut apple, melatonin plus selenium nanoparticles in soybean, and an SOD review are related but less module-centered.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-24"
}
